# Overview
#
# A hedge is a word that marks a claim as a possibility, such as `might`
# or `possibly`. A double hedge stacks two hedges on one claim (`could
# potentially fail`). The second hedge repeats the first and tells the
# reader nothing more. The prose skill keeps `might` for a possibility,
# so the fix writes `might` in place of both words. The rule sits at
# Required, because it matches exact pairs of words and the fix is
# mechanical.
#
# Expression
#
# The pattern pairs `may`, `might`, `could`, or `can` with `possibly` or
# `potentially`. `let becomes` rewrites the pair to `might`.
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseRedundantPair leaves these pairs out, because its
# fix deletes one word, and `may` or `could` alone then draws a finding
# from ProseMayPossibility or ProseModal. Both of those rules skip a
# following `possibly` or `potentially`, so only this rule reports the
# pair.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 17 lines in the measure.py corpus, and in 15 of 17
# sampled hits `might` fit. In the other two, `can potentially` named an
# ability (`can potentially meet its scheduling policy criteria`), where
# `can` fits.
expr main ([may, might, could, can] [possibly, potentially])
let message "Double hedge. Write `might`, or `can` for an ability."
let description "Flags `could potentially` and similar pairs that mark one possibility twice."
let becomes "might"
let kind "Style"
test "The write could potentially fail on a full disk." "The write might fail on a full disk."
allows "A potentially unsafe call fails the build."
allows "The write might fail on a full disk."
